## Building

The image for each target of `tools/project.sh` is built by CMake with the toolchain file it
names, its portable code from `src/` and what the target needs from `platform/<name>/`:

```bash
make examples    # the image for each target, its flash and RAM as a table
```

The image of the first target lies in `build/targets/{{ mcu }}/default/programs/{{ project_name }}.elf`.
{% else %}
## Installing
{% if arduino %}
Clone it into the `libraries` folder of your sketchbook, under the name `{{ project_name }}`:

```bash
git clone {{ url }} ~/Arduino/libraries/{{ project_name }}
```

It is built and checked for `{{ fqbn }}`.

## Using it

```cpp
#include <{{ project_name }}.h>

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  Serial.println({{ namespace }}::Clamp(analogRead(A0), 100, 900));
  delay(1000);
}
```
{% else %}
Its headers lie in `src/`; a program puts that folder on its include path, or takes the library
with CMake:

```cmake
add_subdirectory({{ project_name }})
```

It is built and checked for the `{{ mcu }}` by avr-gcc, without the Arduino core.

## Using it

```cpp
#include <{{ project_name }}.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int16_t Limited(int16_t reading) { return {{ namespace }}::Clamp<int16_t>(reading, 100, 900); }
```
{% endif %}
The examples in [examples/](examples) show more; the reference is built from the Doxygen blocks
of `src/`, see [docs/Documentation.md](docs/Documentation.md).
{% endif %}
## Developing

The dev container in [.devcontainer/](.devcontainer/README.md) carries every tool. `make` lists
every build and check, the same CI runs:

```bash
{%- if kind == 'library' and arduino %}
make setup    # the pinned Arduino core, once
{%- else %}
make setup    # checks the toolchain file of each target, once
{%- endif %}
make lint     # the checks of seconds; make fmt formats
make tests    # the tests on the host
make check    # all of it, warnings as errors, as CI
```

A table of the {% if kind == 'firmware' %}image's{% else %}examples'{% endif %} flash and RAM in this README, between the lines `<!-- sizes -->` and
`<!-- /sizes -->`, is checked against the build: `make check` fails where it differs from what
`make examples` measures, and `make sizes FIX=1` writes the measured table in.

The checks, the CI and the dev container come from the
[template](https://github.com/theAndreas/embedded-cpp-template); `uv run copier update` brings
its newest version. What is the project's own for them lies in `tools/project.sh` and
`tools/project.mk`.

